  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four coral-reef states under the same climate stressors—high temperature and low pH—and shows how community composition, coral cover, and net calcium carbonate production change from worst to best condition. In the “extreme” and “degraded” reefs, coral cover is very low, and the reef is dominated by scattered encrusting coralline algae, sponges, some surviving hard corals, and more non-coral invertebrates such as barnacles, mussels, annelids, and sea urchins. These states have the most negative CaCO3 balance, indicating strong reef erosion or loss of framework.\n\nBy contrast, the “resilient” reef has the highest coral cover, a denser assemblage of hard corals and weedy corals, and the least negative to slightly positive carbonate production. It is marked as benefiting from natural resistance despite the same heat and acidification pressures. The “restored” reef sits between degraded and resilient conditions: active intervention appears to rebuild coral communities and improve reef growth compared with degraded states, but not to the level of the naturally resilient reef.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that reef outcomes under warming and acidification depend strongly on ecological condition and management, with resilience or restoration reducing net carbonate loss.",
